TI  - A 70-year-old man with intraoperative hypoxia and hypotension during total hip 
      replacement.
PG  - e160-e162
LID - S0012-3692(15)52418-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.14-0411 [doi]
AB  - A 70-year-old man underwent total hip replacement surgery under general 
      anesthesia, endotracheal intubation, and controlled ventilation, with the patient 
      in left lateral position. Intraoperatively, the patient was hemodynamically 
      stable, with a normal range of pulse, ECG trace, oxygen saturation as measured by 
      pulse oximetry (Spo2), and end-tidal CO2 (etco2). Five minutes after insertion of 
      the hip prosthesis, his heart rate dropped to 30 beats/min, BP to 40/30 mm Hg, 
      Spo2 to 70%, and etco2 to 10 mm Hg.
