TI  - A 62-year-old women with persistent severe asthma, skin rash, and eosinophilia.
PG  - e52-e55
LID - S0012-3692(15)48848-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.14-0347 [doi]
AB  - A 62-year-old white woman was admitted with shortness of breath, wheezing, and 
      cough. While in the hospital a generalized pruritic skin rash developed on her 
      trunk and upper and lower extremities. She did not have any fevers, chills, or 
      night sweats. The patient was known to have chronic, difficult-to-control asthma 
      despite being compliant with a treatment regimen consisting of inhaled albuterol, 
      high-dose inhaled steroids, salmeterol, and montelukast. Her medical history was 
      significant for hypertension and gout. She had no family history of asthma. The 
      patient was a life-long nonsmoker and did not drink alcohol. During this 
      hospitalization, she was started on prednisone 40 mg/d po in addition to her home 
      medications.
