TI  - Obesity and right ventricular structure and function: the MESA-Right Ventricle 
      Study.
PG  - 388-395
LID - S0012-3692(12)60076-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.11-0172 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The relationship between obesity and right ventricular (RV) 
      morphology is not well studied. We aimed to determine the association between 
      obesity and RV structure and function in a large multiethnic population-based 
      cohort. METHODS: The MESA-Right Ventricle Study measured RV mass and volumes by 
      cardiac MRI in participants aged 45 to 84 years without clinical cardiovascular 
      disease in the Multi-Ethnic Study of Atherosclerosis (MESA). Participants were 
      divided into three categories based on BMI: lean ( ≤ 24.9 kg/m(2)), overweight 
      (25-29.9 kg/m(2)), and obese ( ≥ 30 kg/m(2)). RESULTS: The study sample included 
      4,127 participants. After adjustment for demographics, height, education, and 
      cardiovascular risk factors, overweight and obese participants had greater RV 
      mass (6% and 9% greater, respectively), larger RV end-diastolic volume (8% and 
      18% greater, respectively), larger RV stroke volume (7% and 16% greater, 
      respectively), and lower RV ejection fraction ( ≥ 1% lower) than lean 
      participants (all P < .001). These findings persisted after adjusting for the 
      respective left ventricular (LV) parameters. CONCLUSIONS: Overweight and obesity 
      were independently associated with differences in RV morphology even after 
      adjustment for the respective LV measure. This association could be explained by 
      increased RV afterload, increased blood volume, hormonal effects, or direct 
      obesity-related myocardial effects.
