DP  - 2012 Sep
TI  - Obesity and ARDS.
PG  - 785-790
LID - S0012-3692(12)60526-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.12-0117 [doi]
AB  - Obesity prevalence continues to increase globally, with figures exceeding 30% of 
      some populations. Patients who are obese experience alterations in baseline 
      pulmonary mechanics, including airflow obstruction, decreased lung volumes, and 
      impaired gas exchange. These physiologic changes have implications in many 
      diseases, including ARDS. The unique physiology of patients who are obese affects 
      the presentation and pathophysiology of ARDS, and patients who are obese who have 
      respiratory failure present specific management challenges. Although more study 
      is forthcoming, ventilator strategies that focus on transpulmonary pressure as a 
      measure of lung stress show promise in pilot studies. Given the increasing 
      prevalence of obesity and the variable effects of obesity on respiratory 
      mechanics and ARDS pathophysiology, we recommend an individualized approach to 
      the management of the obese patient with ARDS.
FAU - Hibbert, Kathryn
