TI  - Translational educational research: a necessity for effective health-care 
      improvement.
PG  - 1097-1103
LID - S0012-3692(12)60609-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1378/chest.12-0148 [doi]
AB  - Medical education research contributes to translational science (TS) when its 
      outcomes not only impact educational settings, but also downstream results, 
      including better patient-care practices and improved patient outcomes. 
      Simulation-based medical education (SBME) has demonstrated its role in achieving 
      such distal results. Effective TS also encompasses implementation science, the 
      science of health-care delivery. Educational, clinical, quality, and safety goals 
      can only be achieved by thematic, sustained, and cumulative research programs, 
      not isolated studies. Components of an SBME TS research program include motivated 
      learners, curriculum grounded in evidence-based learning theory, educational 
      resources, evaluation of downstream results, a productive research team, rigorous 
      research methods, research resources, and health-care system acceptance and 
      implementation. National research priorities are served from translational 
      educational research. National funding priorities should endorse the contribution 
      and value of translational education research.
